      "id": 231102,
      "name": "doppelgangerung",
      "username": "doppelgangerung",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/doppelgangerung/{size}/1876_2.png",
      "created_at": "2020-06-24T21:16:58.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"FrivYeti\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/frivyeti/48/1919_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> FrivYeti:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This actually brings up an interesting question. If you’re in Oblivaeon, and Fanatic has, say, the T-Rex bot in play, and Fanatic doesn’t do any damage but the T-Rex bot chomps someone for 9 damage, did <em>I </em>do three damage for keeping Zealous Offense around? Should it be keyed to Fanatic, or to cards in Fanatic’s play area? Has someone tested what the game’s decision was on this one?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>My guess would be that the damage is presumed to come from wherever the card says it comes from - just as an effect that prevents a hero card from doing damage does not prevent damage from a card in that hero’s play area, provided the card specified that <em>it</em> is doing the damage.</p>\n<p>That is probably bad news for Zealous Offense fans.</p>",
